WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.503
Hello, everyone! I'm Mr. Daniel. Welcome to Online Upper-Intermediate.

2
00:00:04.803 --> 00:00:07.922
Hi! I'm Nita. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.222 --> 00:00:15.321
Unit twenty-nine: Disaster and Response. In this unit, you will describe emergencies and humanitarian action.

4
00:00:16.317 --> 00:00:17.654
humanitarian aid.

5
00:00:18.354 --> 00:00:22.688
humanitarian aid means help such as food and medicine for people in a crisis.

6
00:00:22.988 --> 00:00:25.510
Humanitarian aid arrived within two days.

7
00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:28.827
Your turn. Say: humanitarian aid.

8
00:00:31.527 --> 00:00:32.993
humanitarian aid.

9
00:00:33.983 --> 00:00:35.228
evacuation.

10
00:00:35.928 --> 00:00:39.542
evacuation means moving people away from a dangerous place.

11
00:00:39.842 --> 00:00:42.561
The evacuation of the village took six hours.

12
00:00:43.261 --> 00:00:45.948
Your turn. Say: evacuation.

13
00:00:48.648 --> 00:00:49.835
evacuation.

14
00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:51.781
relief effort.

15
00:00:52.481 --> 00:00:56.455
relief effort means organised work to help people after a disaster.

16
00:00:56.755 --> 00:00:59.172
Thousands of volunteers joined the relief effort.

17
00:00:59.872 --> 00:01:02.525
Your turn. Say: relief effort.

18
00:01:05.225 --> 00:01:06.411
relief effort.

19
00:01:07.417 --> 00:01:08.441
early warning.

20
00:01:09.141 --> 00:01:12.719
early warning means information about danger given before it happens.

21
00:01:13.019 --> 00:01:15.449
An early warning saved many lives.

22
00:01:16.149 --> 00:01:18.604
Your turn. Say: early warning.

23
00:01:21.304 --> 00:01:22.432
early warning.

24
00:01:23.417 --> 00:01:24.871
disaster preparedness.

25
00:01:25.571 --> 00:01:29.532
disaster preparedness means being ready for a disaster before it happens.

26
00:01:29.832 --> 00:01:32.540
Schools teach disaster preparedness every year.

27
00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:35.799
Your turn. Say: disaster preparedness.

28
00:01:38.499 --> 00:01:40.151
disaster preparedness.

29
00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:42.198
aftershock.

30
00:01:42.898 --> 00:01:45.861
aftershock means a smaller earthquake that follows a big one.

31
00:01:46.161 --> 00:01:48.404
Several aftershocks were felt during the night.

32
00:01:49.104 --> 00:01:51.664
Your turn. Say: aftershock.

33
00:01:54.364 --> 00:01:55.492
aftershock.

34
00:01:56.483 --> 00:01:57.542
tsunami warning.

35
00:01:58.242 --> 00:02:02.309
tsunami warning means an alert that a giant wave may hit the coast.

36
00:02:02.609 --> 00:02:05.316
A tsunami warning was issued after the earthquake.

37
00:02:06.016 --> 00:02:08.402
Your turn. Say: tsunami warning.

38
00:02:11.102 --> 00:02:12.230
tsunami warning.

39
00:02:13.217 --> 00:02:14.287
flash flood.

40
00:02:14.987 --> 00:02:18.461
flash flood means a sudden flood caused by heavy rain.

41
00:02:18.761 --> 00:02:20.877
A flash flood blocked the mountain road.

42
00:02:21.577 --> 00:02:24.183
Your turn. Say: flash flood.

43
00:02:26.883 --> 00:02:27.907
flash flood.

44
00:02:28.917 --> 00:02:30.974
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:31.274 --> 00:02:34.275
I heard there was a flash flood in the north. What happened?

46
00:02:35.583 --> 00:02:39.742
It had been raining for three days when the river suddenly rose.

47
00:02:41.050 --> 00:02:42.724
Was there an early warning?

48
00:02:44.017 --> 00:02:48.747
Yes. People were being evacuated while the water was still rising.

49
00:02:50.050 --> 00:02:51.597
How did the relief effort go?

50
00:02:52.883 --> 00:02:57.019
Humanitarian aid had already been prepared, so it arrived quickly.

51
00:02:58.317 --> 00:03:01.152
Grammar focus: Narrative tenses (advanced).

52
00:03:01.452 --> 00:03:04.032
Advanced narratives combine several tenses.

53
00:03:04.332 --> 00:03:12.392
Past perfect continuous for long background, past continuous passive for actions in progress, past perfect passive for things completed earlier.

54
00:03:12.692 --> 00:03:16.816
For example: It had been raining for days, and people were being evacuated.

55
00:03:17.116 --> 00:03:21.554
Mixing these tenses makes stories and news reports more precise and dramatic.

56
00:03:22.550 --> 00:03:25.237
Now read and listen. Ready Before the Storm.

57
00:03:25.537 --> 00:03:30.219
In 2004, few coastal towns in Asia had heard a tsunami warning before.

58
00:03:31.517 --> 00:03:34.933
Since then, early warning systems have been built across the region.

59
00:03:36.217 --> 00:03:41.154
When an earthquake struck in 2018, people were being moved inland within minutes.

60
00:03:42.450 --> 00:03:46.679
Emergency supplies had been prepared, and the relief effort began immediately.

61
00:03:47.983 --> 00:03:52.525
Disaster preparedness turned a potential tragedy into a successful evacuation.

62
00:03:53.817 --> 00:03:56.272
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

63
00:03:56.572 --> 00:03:58.908
Moving people away from danger is blank.

64
00:03:59.208 --> 00:04:02.977
A, evacuation. B, aftershock. C, relief effort.

65
00:04:06.983 --> 00:04:08.902
The answer is A: evacuation.

66
00:04:10.417 --> 00:04:13.008
They blank for hours when the rescue team arrived.

67
00:04:13.308 --> 00:04:16.370
A, had been waiting. B, have waited. C, wait.

68
00:04:20.383 --> 00:04:22.278
The answer is A: had been waiting.

69
00:04:23.783 --> 00:04:25.551
The road blank when we got there.

70
00:04:25.851 --> 00:04:29.829
A, was being repaired. B, repaired. C, is repairing.

71
00:04:33.817 --> 00:04:36.188
The answer is A: was being repaired.

72
00:04:37.683 --> 00:04:40.298
A smaller earthquake after a big one is an blank.

73
00:04:40.598 --> 00:04:44.542
A, aftershock. B, early warning. C, flash flood.

74
00:04:48.550 --> 00:04:50.422
The answer is A: aftershock.

75
00:04:51.917 --> 00:04:56.854
According to the text, what turned a potential tragedy into a successful evacuation?

76
00:04:57.154 --> 00:05:00.923
A, luck. B, disaster preparedness. C, the weather.

77
00:05:04.917 --> 00:05:07.520
The answer is B: disaster preparedness.

78
00:05:09.017 --> 00:05:13.561
Now it's your turn to speak. How can communities prepare for natural disasters?

79
00:05:18.861 --> 00:05:24.948
They should have early warning systems, practise evacuations and keep emergency supplies ready.

80
00:05:25.248 --> 00:05:26.713
Excellent answer, Nita!

81
00:05:27.717 --> 00:05:31.855
Great work today! You learned eight new words and narrative tenses (advanced).

82
00:05:32.155 --> 00:05:35.365
Today's value is being prepared. Get ready before you go.

83
00:05:35.665 --> 00:05:37.922
See you in Unit thirty. Goodbye!

84
00:05:38.222 --> 00:05:40.828
Thank you, Mr. Daniel! Bye, everyone!

